  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1217 PM MDT Mon Aug 10 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge will continue to dominate the weather today \nand tomorrow, before a pattern change brings relatively cooler \ntemperatures and increased chances in precipitation chances \nbeginning Wednesday. Today will be similar to what we have been \nseeing over the weekend, with temperatures in the mid-to-upper \n90s and scattered showers and thunderstorms for the mountains and \nplains. Current satellite imagery shows that cumulus has \ndeveloped, with virga showers already popping up across mountains \nand portions of the I-25 corridor. ACARS soundings still show a \nvery dry airmass at the low-levels, so expect most of the showers \nto produce little to no rainfall for the plains. However, with the\nincrease in moisture, a few of the stronger storms likely will \nproduce light rainfall amounts. SPC mesoanalysis page has DCAPE \nvalues ranging from 900-1300 J/kg, so gusty winds of 30-45 mph (a \nfew could be stronger) will be a concern this afternoon/evening. \n\nAs these storms track east later this afternoon/evening, they will \nbe tapping into a better environment (better low-level moisture, and \nincreased instability) that could support strong to severe storms. \nCAMs this morning favor storms becoming more linear as they reach\nthe eastern plains, closer to Logan/Washington counties. Outflow \ngusts of 60-70 mph are possible, with a lower threat for hail. SPC\nupgraded portions of the eastern plains to a Slight Risk (2/5) \nfor this reason. \n\nTuesday will be similar, with hot temperatures in the mid/upper 90s \nand high-based showers and thunderstorms in the afternoon. However, \ninstability does not look as favorable, so only expecting outflow \ngusts up to 35 mph with isolated stronger. We should see a bit \nmore storms having rain reach the ground due continued increase in\nmid- level moisture. Guidance has this mostly confined to the \nmountains and high valleys due to better moisture availability. \n\nBy Wednesday, the upper level ridge will weaken and shift east as\na weak shortwave embedded in the flow will traverse the region \nWednesday evening/night. Southwest flow aloft will advect \nmonsoonal moisture into the region, where our mountains will \nreceive more widespread showers and thunderstorms. For the \nplains, scattered showers/thunderstorms will occur, with the \npossibility of some becoming strong-to-severe, particularly for \nthe eastern plains. Modeled Skew-T soundings show very favorable \nshear profiles, with decent instability. SPC introduced a Slight \nRisk (2/5) for this reason, which seems reasonable at this time. \nSteep low/mid-level lapse rates will support strong outflow winds,\nhowever there is also a hail threat. Precipitable water values \nare expected to get up to 1- 1.3\", so any strong storm has the \nability to produce localized flooding concerns. Storm motion \nshould be fast, so widespread flooding will not be a concern as of\nright now. \n\nFor Thursday, more monsoonal moisture will arrive, with precipitable \nwater values increasing to 120-170% of normal. The upper level \nshortwave will continue to trek across Colorado Thursday morning, \nproviding lift needed to generate more widespread beneficial \nrainfall for the plains. Modeled soundings depict a favorable \nprofile for efficient precipitation (above average PWAT, deep warm\ncloud layer, moist vertical profile) thus there is a higher \nthreat for flooding. WPC has a Slight Risk for excessive rainfall\nfor much of the mountains, I-25 Corridor, and plains for this \nreason. \n\nFriday and into the weekend, ensemble guidance continues to look \npromising in keeping the upper level ridge out our southeast. \nThis will keep us relatively cool (highs in the 80s) with daily \nchances of afternoon showers and thunderstorms.",
